From f738f888bc8839e3ff896efa0cc8abf5dfa47cf9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 01:42:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] microblaze: keep code alignment when relaxing

Linker relaxation deletes IMM instructions, and everything after a
deleted IMM in the section moves down by 4 bytes.  Nothing kept track
of alignment padding, and the assembler emitted no relocation for it,
so labels after ".balign 16" or ".p2align 4", and functions compiled
with -falign-functions, lost their alignment.  In the reproducer, a
call to another section followed by ".balign 16" puts aligned16 at
0x100c instead of 0x1010; in a C program built with -O2
-falign-functions=32 three of four functions came out misaligned.

Mark alignment padding in code with a new relocation,
R_MICROBLAZE_ALIGN, as RISC-V and LoongArch do with R_RISCV_ALIGN and
R_LARCH_ALIGN.  Its offset is the start of the padding, and its addend
holds the size of the padding and the base-2 logarithm of the
alignment.  Unlike those targets, the assembler keeps emitting just the
padding the alignment needs, not the worst case, so a link without
relaxation is unchanged and needs nothing from the linker.  The
assembler marks only alignments above 4 bytes, since relaxation deletes
whole instructions; it marks padding skipped because of a .balign
maximum with a size of 0, and the linker leaves that alone.

When relaxation deletes D bytes before an alignment point with PAD bytes
of padding, the target stays aligned if the padding shrinks to
(PAD + D) mod ALIGN bytes.  When that is more than PAD, padding would
have to be inserted.  Rather than grow sections, relaxation then keeps
IMMs before the point, latest first, until no padding needs to grow; it
deletes the padding it can.  Keeping every IMM before a point makes D
zero, so this always succeeds, and since relaxation still only
deletes, no address and no distance increases, which the range checks
of earlier passes rely on.  The candidate IMMs of a pass are now
collected first and the deletions chosen afterwards, in a new function,
microblaze_elf_choose_deletions, and the relaxation table can now hold
deletions of any multiple of 4 bytes.

R_MICROBLAZE_ALIGN is 46, the next free number.  An older linker
rejects it, so objects with code alignment from the new assembler need
the new linker.

With this change the C program above, linked with --relax and run under
QEMU, reports all functions 32-byte aligned and computes the same
result as with --no-relax; its .text is the same size as with the old
linker.

